.TH ORO-APPLICATION 3 "" "Oro Runtime" "Oro Runtime API Manual"
.SH NAME
oro:application \- JavaScript API module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
import * as module from 'oro:application'
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Provides Application level methods
.PP
Example usage: import { createWindow } from 'oro:application'
.SH REFERENCE
.TP
\fBMAX_WINDOWS\fR
Maximum number of concurrently tracked application windows.
.br
The runtime currently caps window indices at this value when enumerating or creating windows through the high\-level application APIs.
.TP
\fBApplicationWindowList\fR
Ordered collection of `ApplicationWindow` instances keyed by window index.
.br
The list is iterable, preserves ascending window\-index order, and also exposes each window at `list[window.index]` for direct indexed lookup.
.TP
\fBfrom(args)\fR
Creates a window list from either a single array or variadic window arguments.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIargs\fR (\&...ApplicationWindow \\| ApplicationWindow[]; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIApplicationWindowList\fR
.TP
\fBconstructor(items)\fR
Documented method exposed by this module.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIitems\fR (ApplicationWindow[]; optional)
.TP
\fBlength()\fR
Number of windows currently stored in the list.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fInumber\fR
.TP
\fBsize()\fR
Alias for `length`.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fInumber\fR
.TP
\fBforEach(callback, thisArg)\fR
Invokes `callback` once for each window in the list.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIcallback\fR ((window: ApplicationWindow, index: number, list: ApplicationWindow[]) => void; required)
.br
\fIthisArg\fR (any; optional)
.TP
\fBitem(index)\fR
Returns the window stored at `index`, if present.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIindex\fR (number; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIApplicationWindow \\| undefined\fR
.TP
\fBentries()\fR
Returns `[window.index, window]` pairs for the current list contents.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIArray<[number, ApplicationWindow]>\fR
.TP
\fBkeys()\fR
Returns the ordered window indices contained in the list.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fInumber[]\fR
.TP
\fBvalues()\fR
Returns the ordered window instances contained in the list.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIApplicationWindow[]\fR
.TP
\fBadd(window)\fR
Inserts or replaces a window in the list using its `window.index`.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIwindow\fR (ApplicationWindow; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIApplicationWindowList\fR
.TP
\fBremove(windowOrIndex)\fR
Removes a window from the list by instance or numeric index.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIwindowOrIndex\fR (ApplicationWindow \\| number; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIboolean\fR
.TP
\fBcontains(windowOrIndex)\fR
Returns `true` when the list contains a window for the given instance or numeric index.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIwindowOrIndex\fR (ApplicationWindow \\| number; required)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIboolean\fR
.TP
\fBclear()\fR
Removes all windows from the list.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIApplicationWindowList\fR
.TP
\fBaddEventListener(type, listener, options)\fR
Add an application event `type` callback `listener` with `options`.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fItype\fR (string; required)
.br
\fIlistener\fR (function(Event \\| MessageEvent \\| CustomEvent \\| ApplicationURLEvent): boolean; required)
.br
\fIoptions\fR ({ once?: boolean } \\| boolean; optional; default null)
.TP
\fBremoveEventListener(type, listener)\fR
Remove an application event `type` callback `listener` with `options`.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fItype\fR (string; required)
.br
\fIlistener\fR (function(Event \\| MessageEvent \\| CustomEvent \\| ApplicationURLEvent): boolean; required)
.TP
\fBgetCurrentWindowIndex()\fR
Returns the current window index
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fInumber\fR
.TP
\fBcreateWindow(opts)\fR
Creates a new window and returns an instance of ApplicationWindow.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIopts\fR (object; required) an options object
.br
\fIopts.aspectRatio\fR (string; optional) a string (split on ':') provides two float values which set the window's aspect ratio.
.br
\fIopts.closable\fR (boolean; optional) deterime if the window can be closed.
.br
\fIopts.minimizable\fR (boolean; optional) deterime if the window can be minimized.
.br
\fIopts.maximizable\fR (boolean; optional) deterime if the window can be maximized.
.br
\fIopts.margin\fR (number; optional) a margin around the webview. (Private)
.br
\fIopts.radius\fR (number; optional) a radius on the webview. (Private)
.br
\fIopts.index\fR (number; optional; default \-1) the index of the window, if not provided or the value is `\-1`, then one will be assigned
.br
\fIopts.path\fR (string; required) the path to the HTML file to load into the window.
.br
\fIopts.title\fR (string; optional) the title of the window.
.br
\fIopts.titlebarStyle\fR (string; optional) determines the style of the titlebar (MacOS only).
.br
\fIopts.windowControlOffsets\fR (string; optional) a string (split on 'x') provides the x and y position of the traffic lights (MacOS only).
.br
\fIopts.backgroundColorDark\fR (string; optional) determines the background color of the window in dark mode.
.br
\fIopts.backgroundColorLight\fR (string; optional) determines the background color of the window in light mode.
.br
\fIopts.followSystemTheme\fR (boolean; optional) whether the window should follow the desktop theme (default: true).
.br
\fIopts.preferDarkTheme\fR (boolean; optional) whether the window should prefer a dark theme when not following the system theme.
.br
\fIopts.width\fR (number \\| string; optional) the width of the window. If undefined, the window will have the main window width.
.br
\fIopts.height\fR (number \\| string; optional) the height of the window. If undefined, the window will have the main window height.
.br
\fIopts.minWidth\fR (number \\| string; optional; default 0) the minimum width of the window
.br
\fIopts.minHeight\fR (number \\| string; optional; default 0) the minimum height of the window
.br
\fIopts.maxWidth\fR (number \\| string; optional; default '100%') the maximum width of the window
.br
\fIopts.maxHeight\fR (number \\| string; optional; default '100%') the maximum height of the window
.br
\fIopts.resizable\fR (boolean; optional; default true) whether the window is resizable
.br
\fIopts.frameless\fR (boolean; optional; default false) whether the window is frameless
.br
\fIopts.utility\fR (boolean; optional; default false) whether the window is utility (macOS only)
.br
\fIopts.shouldExitApplicationOnClose\fR (boolean; optional; default false) whether the window can exit the app
.br
\fIopts.headless\fR (boolean; optional; default false) whether the window will be headless or not (no frame)
.br
\fIopts.userScript\fR (string; optional; default null) A user script that will be injected into the window (desktop only)
.br
\fIopts.protocolHandlers\fR (string[]; optional) An array of protocol handler schemes to register with the new window (requires service worker)
.br
\fIopts.config\fR (Record<string, string \\| number \\| boolean \\| (string \\| number \\| boolean)[]>; optional) additional configuration key/value pairs
.br
\fIopts.resourcesDirectory\fR (string; optional)
.br
\fIopts.shouldPreferServiceWorker\fR (boolean; optional; default false)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ApplicationWindow>\fR
.TP
\fBgetScreenSize()\fR
Returns the current screen size.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<{ width: number, height: number }>\fR
.TP
\fBgetWindows(indices, options)\fR
Returns the ApplicationWindow instances for the given indices or all windows if no indices are provided.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIindices\fR (number[]; optional) the indices of the windows
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ApplicationWindowQueryOptions; optional; default null)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ApplicationWindowList>\fR
.TP
\fBgetWindow(index, options)\fR
Returns the ApplicationWindow instance for the given index
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIindex\fR (number; required) the index of the window
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ApplicationWindowQueryOptions; optional)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ApplicationWindow \\| undefined>\fR the ApplicationWindow instance or `undefined` if the window does not exist
.TP
\fBgetCurrentWindow()\fR
Returns the ApplicationWindow instance for the current window.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ApplicationWindow>\fR
.TP
\fBexit(code)\fR
Quits the backend process and then quits the render process, the exit code used is the final exit code to the OS.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIcode\fR (number; optional; default 0) an exit code
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ipc.Result['data']>\fR
.TP
\fBsetSystemMenu(options)\fR
Set the native menu for the app.
.br
Oro Runtime provides a minimalist DSL that makes it easy to create cross platform native system and context menus.
.br
Menus are created at run time. They can be created from either the Main or Render process. The can be recreated instantly by calling the `setSystemMenu` method.
.br
The method takes a string. Here's an example of a menu. The semi colon is significant indicates the end of the menu. Use an underscore when there is no accelerator key. Modifiers are optional. And well known OS menu options like the edit menu will automatically get accelerators you dont need to specify them.
.br
oro.application.setSystemMenu({ index: 0, value: ` App: Foo: f;
.br
Edit: Cut: x Copy: c Paste: v Delete: _ Select All: a;
.br
Other: Apple: _ Another Test: T !Im Disabled: I Some Thing: S + Meta \-\-\- Bazz: s + Meta, Control, Alt; `)
.br
Separators
.br
To create a separator, use three dashes `\-\-\-`.
.br
Accelerator Modifiers
.br
Accelerator modifiers are used as visual indicators but don't have a material impact as the actual key binding is done in the event listener.
.br
A capital letter implies that the accelerator is modified by the `Shift` key.
.br
Additional accelerators are `Meta`, `Control`, `Option`, each separated by commas. If one is not applicable for a platform, it will just be ignored.
.br
On MacOS `Meta` is the same as `Command`.
.br
Disabled Items
.br
If you want to disable a menu item just prefix the item with the `!` character. This will cause the item to appear disabled when the system menu renders.
.br
Submenus
.br
We feel like nested menus are an anti\-pattern. We don't use them. If you have a strong argument for them and a very simple pull request that makes them work we may consider them.
.br
Event Handling
.br
When a menu item is activated, it raises the `menuItemSelected` event in the front end code, you can then communicate with your backend code if you want from there.
.br
For example, if the `Apple` item is selected from the `Other` menu...
.br
window.addEventListener('menuItemSelected', event => { assert(event.detail.parent === 'Other') assert(event.detail.title === 'Apple') })
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ApplicationMenuOptions; required) an options object
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ipc.Result>\fR
.TP
\fBsetTrayMenu(options)\fR
An alias to `setSystemMenu()` for creating a tray menu.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIoptions\fR (ApplicationMenuOptions; required) an options object
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ipc.Result>\fR
.TP
\fBsetSystemMenuItemEnabled(value)\fR
Set the enabled state of the system menu.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIvalue\fR (object; required) an options object
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ipc.Result>\fR
.TP
\fBisPaused()\fR
Predicate function to determine if application is in a "paused" state.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIboolean\fR
.TP
\fBruntimeVersion\fR
Oro Runtime semantic version metadata mirrored from `process.versions.oro`. The legacy `process.versions.socket` string remains frozen for compatibility.
.TP
\fBdebug\fR
Runtime debug flag.
.TP
\fBconfig\fR
Application configuration.
.TP
\fBbackend\fR
The application's backend instance.
.TP
\fBopen(opts)\fR
Documented property exposed by this module.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIopts\fR (object; required) an options object
.br
\fIopts.force\fR (boolean; optional; default false) whether to force the existing process to close
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ipc.Result>\fR
.TP
\fBclose()\fR
Documented property exposed by this module.
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<ipc.Result>\fR
